```cpp
class SpinlockB {
    std::atomic<bool> held{false};
public:
    void lock() {
        bool expected = false;
        while (!held.compare_exchange_strong(expected, true,
                    std::memory_order_acquire, std::memory_order_relaxed)) {
            expected = false;        // CAS failure wrote the observed value
        }
    }
    void unlock() { held.store(false, std::memory_order_release); }
};
```
**(1)** The CAS success ordering is **acquire**: the winning thread must synchronise with the previous holder's **release** store in unlock, so everything the previous holder did inside the section happens-before this holder's work. The failure ordering can be relaxed: a failed CAS means we must retry and we have not read anything ordered-critical yet (it is the *successful* acquisition that consumes the previous release). **(2)** On failure, `compare_exchange_strong` loads the observed value into `expected`; after the first failure `expected` is `true`, and without resetting it the loop retries CAS(false->true) against a flag that is true - it can only fail again once the flag actually clears, but with `expected` stuck at true every attempt compares wrong; the reset restores the intended 'try to acquire an unheld lock' semantics. **(3)** With `store(relaxed)` there is no release edge, so the next successful acquire-CAS synchronises with nothing: writes made inside the previous critical section are not guaranteed visible to the new holder. Mutual exclusion survives; visibility does not. The 'saved fence' buys microseconds and costs a rare, unreproducible stale-read bug - exactly the trade a correctness argument exists to catch.

